course: dictionary
lesson: 226
title: Claims, not facts
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words show how sure the writer is. Allegedly means people say something is true, but nobody has proved it yet. «The man allegedly stole the bike» means someone has claimed he took it, and the writer is not saying it for certain.
explain: News reporters and police use allegedly so they do not state that someone is guilty before a court decides. «The actor was allegedly seen at the party» tells you that someone reports it, not that it has been checked. Read it as a claim and keep an open mind.
explain: Allegedly is a warning word in rumours and fake news. People also use it as a joke after a claim they do not quite believe. Ask yourself: who is saying this, and has anyone proved it?
fact: Allegedly means people say something is true, but it has not been proved.
fact: Allegedly is an adverb, so it tells you how sure the writer is about an action.
fact: «Allegedly stole», «was allegedly seen» and «allegedly said» are common phrases in the news.
fact: Reporters and police use allegedly so they do not say for certain that someone did something wrong before a court decides.
fact: Informal use: people sometimes say "allegedly" jokingly to show they doubt a claim.
exercise: «The woman allegedly took the money.» What does allegedly tell the reader? | It was a happy surprise | *It is a claim that has not been proved | It is definitely true, because a court said so | It happened many years ago
exercise: «The driver was allegedly speeding.» Why would a reporter write allegedly here? | Because the driver was very fast and fit | Because the driver has already been found guilty | Because the road was closed | *Because the speeding has not been proved
exercise: «The school allegedly lost the trophy.» Find and copy the word that shows this is a claim. | school | *allegedly | lost | trophy
test: Mia reads, «The shop allegedly sold fake shoes.» What should she remember? | The shop is closed for good | The shop has been proved to sell fake shoes | *It is a claim, and it may not be true | Fake shoes are always sold in shops
test: Why might a police officer say a suspect «allegedly» did something? | Because they saw it happen with their own eyes and are certain | *So they do not claim guilt before a court decides | Because the suspect has already been sentenced | Because it is a joke
exam: Jayden reads, «The singer allegedly quit the band.» What does allegedly mean here? | She quit the band in a joking way that is clearly false | She quit the band and everyone agrees it is true | She never joined the band at all | *People say she quit, but it has not been proved
